            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book entry's assertions are directly and fully supported by the cited scriptures. Genesis 2:8 locates Eden (beginnings) in the east; Ezekiel 43:1-4 and 46:1-3 establish the eastern orientation of the sanctuary and the return of God's glory from that direction; Matthew 24:27 describes Christ's coming as moving from east to west. By grounding the symbol in the Hebrew word 'qedem' (which encapsulates east, front, and ancient/before), the book unites architectural, narrative, and eschatological evidence into a single, cohesive spatial-temporal model (the 'front' from which beginnings and returns come). While a counter-interpretation emphasizes the negative motif of eastward exile, this does not contradict the book's core definition of East as the primary point of origin and return.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The front; the direction from which beginnings (Eden) and divine returns (God's glory, Christ's coming) originate.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Hebrew qedem joins 'east' with 'before' and 'ancient'\",\n    \"Eden is planted eastward\",\n    \"the sanctuary's front door is its east gate\",\n    \"God's glory returns through that gate\",\n    \"Christ's coming moves from east to west\",\n    \"The compass direction is the literal image beneath the symbol\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter claims that prophetic Babylon sits at the 'far west' (hindermost) of the nations, which may conflict with the historical and geographical reality of Babylon being located east of Israel, as well as the prominent prophetic motif of eastern empires as oppressors.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"East is a highly dualistic, context-dependent symbol that equally represents exile, alienation, and destructive judgment (such as the east wind and eastward expulsion from Eden), rather than having a singular core meaning as the positive source of beginnings and returns.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A dualistic symbol representing both divine presence/glory (positive) and human exile/judgment (negative).\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The book's definition selectively highlights the positive associations of 'east' while omitting its frequent use as the direction of exile, estrangement from God, and meteorological destruction.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Genesis 3:24 and 4:16 use eastward movement to depict expulsion from Eden and Cain's exile.\",\n    \"Genesis 11:2 shows the rebellion of Babel occurring through eastward migration.\",\n    \"The 'east wind' is a recurring biblical instrument of destruction and divine judgment (e.g., Exodus 10:13, Jonah 4:8).\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies the architectural orientation of the tabernacle/temple with the narrative origin of Eden and the eschatology of the Second Coming.\",\n    \"Provides a structural linguistic basis (qedem meaning front/east/ancient) that intrinsically links the geographical direction to the concepts of 'beginning' and 'before'.\",\n    \"Explains the specific directionality of divine manifestations (God's glory and Christ's coming moving from east to west).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Accounts for the negative meteorological motif of the east wind.\",\n    \"Includes the narrative pattern of eastward movement as exile and rebellion away from God's ideal.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Genesis 2:8\",\n    \"Ezekiel 43:1-4\",\n    \"Matthew 24:27\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"The tension between 'East' as the positive source of God's presence and 'moving East' as the negative direction of human exile, which the entry does not fully harmonize.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
